[{:seon.task/name "a-probed-entity-is-sought-not-scanned"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by worktree session global-ids (2026-10-23, docs/storage.md section 4) as a bound scalar planning 50x slower than a literal; measured by pure-tests, the scalar is not the cause. On a 116k-datom store the bound and the literal 3-clause point query cost the same (46k and 44k ticks); adding any clause on the form, [?f :form/order ?o] (or :form/ns, :form/id), costs 2.86M ticks, bound or literal, and grows with the store (1.17M at 47k datoms), while a fourth clause on the var costs 51k. The plan (datahike.query/explain) puts [?f :form/src ?src] [?f :form/order ?o] in an ENTITY-GROUP probed by the one ?f the first group binds. Cause: execute-plan-direct passed the consumer group's estimated output rows (:estimated-card g, 279) as execute-group-direct's :scan-estimate, whose rule seeks per probe value only when probe-set-size x probe-driven-threshold < scan size; against 279 that is false, so the whole of :form/src was scanned. Second cause: probe-driven-threshold was the JVM's 2500 (a ~50 us seek against ~20 ns per datom); on Jolt a probe's seek and merge is ~5.6k ticks and a scanned datom ~316, a break-even of N/18, so an attribute under 2,500 datoms was always scanned (329k ticks against 49.5k with the seek, a 12k-datom store). Fixed on Datahike jolt/in-scalar: deaf43a passes the scan op's own estimate at both call sites, 7de92bb makes the threshold 18. The proof's query: 3.24M -> 55k ticks at 10,000 forms."
