  :seon.task/plan "Done 2026-10-27 for the wrong rows (Datahike 63c8e244): the cause was simple-binary-pattern, which gates the fixpoint's delta-driven and magic-set shortcuts, reading only :pushdown-preds; the recursive branch's [(< ?e ?y)] is attached to its scan (:attached-preds: a two-var comparison has no constant to push), so the scan looked bare, the shortcut expanded the edge, and the predicate was never applied (minimal: 10 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 under path with [(< ?e ?y)] answered [10 4] [10 5]). The head var's spelling did not matter. It now takes the plain plan path; proof datahike.test.query-planner-test/a-recursive-branch-applies-its-attached-predicate. Open: the query-work budget below, measured (2026-10-27, Jolt 057b4055): the failing query's two-clause prefix [?f :fn/sym ?sym] [?f :fn/arglists \"([y])\"] with one input symbol costs 201 work units (min :max-work that passes; one clause costs 5), not the 20 the test allows, so the test's 21 was only where the budget tripped. d/explain: ENTITY-GROUP on ?f scans [?f :fn/arglists \"([y])\"] on aevt with est=2 and merges [?f :fn/sym ?sym] est=200. :fn/arglists is not indexed, so a ground value there is a filter over every datom of the attribute (200), and the estimate 2 prices it as if it were an AVET seek; the unique :fn/sym lookup by the input (about 1) is never the scan. The same plan on the JVM would read the same 200 datoms: its sorted-merge cursors (use-cursors? is false on Jolt) do not charge work per datom, which is why the test passes there. Correct: a ground value on an unindexed attribute is estimated at the attribute's datom count, so the group starts from the bound unique lookup and probes arglists by entity (about 3 units); then the test's bound holds on both. Found by the fork-followups session (2026-10-27) once recursive rules ran on Jolt (Jolt's java.util.Arrays/hashCode and HashSet.iterator, jolt/arrays-hashcode-hashset-iterator): datahike.test.query-planner-temporal-test/recursive-rules-answer-like-the-base-engine fails in its :current and :history worlds for the case \"head vars spelled like outer vars\". The query is [:find ?e ?x :in $ % [?id ...] :where [?e :u/id ?id] (path ?e ?x) [?x :u/a ?a]], with path's recursive branch [?e :u/ref ?y] [(< ?e ?y)] [(< ?y 8)] (path ?y ?x), over ring-db (80 entities, then :u/id 1 moved to 81, which refs 3). The base engine (q/*disable-planner* true) answers [81 3] for entity 81 and nothing further, since 81 < 3 is false; the planner also answers [81 5] [81 8] [81 10] [81 11], rows that exist only if the recursive branch's [(< ?e ?y)] was not applied, so the planner answers wrong data. The likely cause is the fixpoint's head var ?e sharing the outer query's spelling, so the predicate reads the outer ?e (a caller's value) or is dropped in the renamed branch. Whether the JVM build answers the same is not known (no JVM run). Correct: the planner answers what the base engine answers in every world. Also in query-planner-test: test-empty-input-relation-reads-nothing exceeds its query-work budget by one (observed 21, allowed 20) on Jolt; read whether the extra unit is a real extra read or Jolt's count."
